  "cause": "**The Knesset convened on Wednesday to seat the Arrangements Committee, and that committee is the effective government until there is one.** **Roy Folkman** of **Yashar** is to chair it, because his party leader **Gadi Eisenkot** holds the mandate from President **Isaac Herzog**; the chairmanship passes to another faction if Eisenkot fails to form a government. Membership is allocated proportionally, and the bloc opposing **Benjamin Netanyahu** won **67 seats** in last month's election — so the committee's majority is the opposition's. Its authority is the part that matters: it can establish temporary and special committees, and it appoints the chairs of the **Finance** and **Foreign Affairs and Defence** committees. Legislation can therefore move before a single minister is sworn. **The chair that was contested last week is now merely scheduled.** A motion to replace Speaker **Amir Ohana** drew the signatures of **67 lawmakers**; Ohana announced that the vote to elect his successor will be held on **Tuesday 17 November at 4 p.m.**, at the opening of the plenum — *\"This evening I received an unusual request from 67 members of the Knesset to place a vote to elect the speaker of the 26th Knesset on the agenda.\"* The bloc's nomination, **Matan Kahana**, is unchanged; the agreement behind it names **Folkman** for the Arrangements Committee and **Avigdor Liberman** for Foreign Affairs and Defence. **United Torah Judaism**'s refusal to vote out Ohana did not become irrelevant so much as beside the point: 67 signatures on a request do not need UTJ's votes, they need the calendar — and the calendar now has a date. **Two clocks are already running through that committee, and neither is about a chair.** The opposition is pressing for a **state commission of inquiry into 7 October** at the new house's first session, per the **NYT** and the **Washington Post**. At the same time **UTJ** is moving to cement draft exemptions for yeshiva students while **B'Yachad** submits a bill expanding benefits for soldiers and reservists — the exemption fight arriving in the same week as the inquiry demand, in a committee that allocates the committees that would hear both. And in the courtroom, Attorney General **Gali Baharav-Miara** declined the trial judges' proposal that she speak with them behind closed doors, while stating that she and State Attorney **Amit Aisman** are *\"open to a direct dialogue for an arrangement\"* — the language of a plea bargain — adding that the prime minister had not answered the president's earlier mediation offer. The judges have twice recommended dropping the bribery charge in **Case 4000**, most recently in June; former attorney general **Avichai Mandelblit**'s testimony has raised questions about the investigation and the indictment. A house that has just taken the agenda is now sitting above a trial that may be looking for an exit.",
